The limit can also be found directly, without memorizing the three cases, as done here.

This calculation uses the fact that for any nonzero number $k$ and positive power $n$, the limit $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}$ is equal to $0$.

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{4x^2-3x}{x^3}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{4x^2-3x}{x^{3}}}{\dfrac{x^3}{x^{3}}}\qquad\gray{\text{Divide both sides by }x^{3}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{4\cancel{x^2}}{x\cdot \cancel{x^2}}-\dfrac{3\cancel x}{\cancel x\cdot x^2}}{\dfrac{\cancel{x^3}}{\cancel{x^3}}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{4}{x}-\dfrac{3}{x^2}}{1}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{0-0}{1}\qquad\gray{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}=0}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{0}{1}
\\\\
&=0
\end{align}$
